//
// Expected: a1 — and the trace must carry the out-of-order note.
//
// Within a phase the attacker's knowledge is atemporal: attacker_phase_update
// seeds every wire value the phase reaches before any principal runs. That is
// what lets an attack route one session's traffic into another regardless of
// block order, and it is load-bearing. This model pins its one bad consequence.
//
// Bob keys w's envelope on a nonce he generates, so a ciphertext under
// HASH(k, n) is the only thing his AEAD_DEC will open. The single ciphertext
// with that shape is v — which Bob himself produces two blocks later, and only
// if the very check being attacked did not halt him first. The engine hands it
// to the attacker anyway, w becomes attacker-authored and successfully used,
// and the authentication query fails.
//
// No other run supplies it either: v is built from Bob's own generated n, so a
// concurrent Bob's v is under HASH(k, n#2) and does not open this one. That is
// the second half of what the note checks, and it is what separates this model
// from exa2.vp and kem_direction_reflection.vp, where the reflected value is on
// the wire before it is replayed and the attack is real.
//
// The verdict stands as a statement about this engine's semantics. What must
// not regress is the note, which is what tells the reader that the witness rests
// on an ordering no run permits.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows public p
]

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	generates n
]

Bob -> Alice: n

principal Alice[
	ka = HASH(k, n)
	w = AEAD_ENC(ka, p, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: w

principal Bob[
	kb = HASH(k, n)
	pb = AEAD_DEC(kb, w, nil)?
]

principal Alice[
	generates u
]

Alice -> Bob: u

principal Bob[
	v = AEAD_ENC(kb, u, nil)
]

Bob -> Alice: v

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: w
]
